  There are 16 types of fish that inhabit the lakes in this game.

     Fish Name          Trophy Size      Maximum Weight
    -----------        -------------    ----------------
  Largemouth Bass          9 lbs             22 lbs
  Walleye                 11 lbs             25 lbs
  Smallmouth Bass          5 lbs             11 lbs
  Northern Pike           17 lbs             46 lbs
  Channel Catfish         25 lbs             58 lbs
  Striped Bass            20 lbs             59 lbs
  Muskellunge  "Musky"    28 lbs             69 lbs
  King Salmon             15 lbs             31 lbs
  Coho Salmon             15 lbs             31 lbs
  Brown Trout             15 lbs             33 lbs
  Lake Trout              20 lbs             65 lbs
  Rainbow Trout            8 lbs             19 lbs

    Panfish
   ---------
  White Bass               3 lbs              5 lbs  Bluegill                 2 lbs              4 lbs  Crappie                  3 lbs              6 lbs  Yellow Perch             2 lbs              4 lbs
                              ]  TOURNAMENT  [

    The weekly tournament is based on a point system.  The number of points
  you receive for a given fish is a percentage of its maximum weight.  An
  example would be an 11 lbs largemouth Bass would be 50 points since its max
  weight is 22 lbs.  If you caught a 22 lbs largemouth Bass it would be 100
  points. 
    This way fish with max weights in the tens and twenties can compete with
  the fish in the forties and fifties.  Especially since 25 lbs Catfish are
  more common than a 22 lbs Bass.
    Depending on when your sysop runs the fishing maintenance the results for
  the week should be decided Saturday morning, meaning a tournament starts
  Saturday and ends Friday.
    Points are given for the best catch on ONE lake at per session. It is not
  accumlitive for a week.  This is so that people that can only play once a
  week can still be in the tournament.

                    ]  Brief Menu Descriptions  [

        You Must Call With ANSI and preferably COLOR.  This version does
     not support ascii but will in the future.

    To start a day of fishing, you must first select a lake that you would
  like to fish.  The map of the lake will then be displayed as well as the
  name and the fishlife it contains.  If you don't care for how the lake
  looks, hit the Space Bar to abort the map.

                                  ] Main Menu [

  (G)o Fishing : This takes you to the Boat Menu (see below) to go fishing. 
      You can return to this menu from the lake menu at any time.  Scores and
      boat position will remain the same.

  (C)hange Lakes : Change from the lake you're in to another lake.  Your limit      and number of fish caught will be reset and any records made on the lake      will be saved before changing lakes. 

  (S)ee Largest fish caught : List of Players, size of fish, and lake the fish      was caught on. 

  (D)isplay Map : Gives you an overhead view of the lake you're in
      and the types of fish that inhabit the lake. 

  (I)nstructions : View these Docs.

  (W)eekly Tournament : Gives you a list of the (3) All Time Point Leaders
      as well as a list of the top (6) sessions of the week.

  (Q)uit : Return to BBS.

              ]  Boat Menu  [

           -=- Boat Movement -=-

                   (8)

                (4)   (6)

                   (2)

             -=- Commands -=-

 Depth Finder :  Keep an eye on this.  This will tell you the depth, and
                 surface and bottom description.  You will need this to
                 help pick your lure.

  (C)ast Lure : This will bring up the casting menu.  Your boat will appear on      the bottom of the screen and you'll cast across the screen to the
      right.  You have two speeds of retrieval to choose from, Fast and Slow.
      You stop the retrieve by hitting [A]bort. For lures with variable
      depths, let the lure sink before hitting [R]etrieve.

      When retrieving a lure you can tell when a fish strikes when the word
      'Strike' appears.  When this happens you have a few moments to 
       hit [H]ook to hook the fish.

      After you have hooked the fish, Hit [R] continuously to real him in.
      Be careful when reeling in fish.  Big fish will sometimes start pulling
      the drag (pull line out from the reel).  You'll know this happens when
      'Whirrrrr!' appears above the fishing line. When this happens, stop 
      reeling by releasing the 'R' key, because he could break the line. 
      If it looks like he is going to go off the screen or just get too
      much line, you can tap the 'R' key and try to hold him a little.
 
      If you are careless and drag the bottom with your lure, you stand
      a chance of getting it snagged and losing it.  You may also even
      pull up trash from the bottom.  If you are in heavy cover, and are
      fishing near the bottom and have a poor lure selected you stand
      a chance of picking up weeds and wood...
      
      When you successfully bring a fish in, the weight of the fish will be 
      displayed.  You can cast again or return to the Boat Menu.

  (P)ick Lure : Takes you to your tackle box to select a lure.  To choose a
      lure select the corresponding number.  A brief description is given for
      each lure below.

                   ----------------------------------------

    Shallow Crankbait : Good in shallow, open water.  Dives to 4' when
                        retrieved slow, 8' when retrieved fast.  Erratic
                        action attracts fish.  Prone to getting snagged when
                        used in heavy cover areas. 

    Deep Crankbait    : Deep open water lure.  Dives to 11' when retrieved
                        slow, 17' when retrieved fast.  Erratic action makes
                        this a good fish attractor, but lure is prone to
                        getting snagged when used in heavy cover.

    Slim Minnow lure  : Good in shallow water.  Dives to 2' when retrieved                        slow, 4' when retrieved fast.  Good on Cloudy days
                        when fish are shallow.

    Surface Plug      : A lure that floats on top of the water and when
                        retrieved makes wakes and noise that attracts fish.
                        Good on Cloudy days when fish are shallow.

    Spinner Bait      : A lure made of wire shaped in a 'V' with a spinner
                        on the top and a leaded hook covered with feathers.
                        This is a very versatile lure.  The flashing spinner
                        is a very good fish attractor.  The lures design
                        makes it almost weedless.  This lure sinks when
                        not moving, stays at the same depth when retrieved
                        slow, and rises in the water when retrieved fast.

    Bottom Bait       : A Plastic Worm is basically a bottom lure being 
                        mistaken for an eel, leech, or just tasty looking.
                        It also does well worked through weeds and other
                        heavy cover.

                   -----------------------------------------

  (L)ook in Livewell  : Displays largest 15 fish caught *BY POINTS* and
                        total number and weight of fish caught on this
                        lake today.

  (Q)uit              : Returns to main menu.  Limit, number of fish caught,
                        and lake position will stay the same if you return.


                               ** HINTS **

   1.  On Sunny Days, fish in deeper water or next to the bottom.
   
   2.  On Cloudy Days, fish near the surface.

   3.  Some areas just don't have fish, if after 4 or 5 casts and you
       still haven't gotten a strike, move on to another location.

   4.  An area can be "fished out", if after you catch several fish in
       an area and haven't gotten a strike in a while, move on.

   5.  To catch a Goodyear Tire, drag the bottom with your lure.  You will
       get points for it but you will never catch a fish dragging the bottom.
       And the chances of getting a tire are less than catching a fish.


